libfprint-egis0576 (1:1.94.100-99egis0.5.0) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Driver v0.5.0, the upstream-ready release: asynchronous I/O (FpiSsm
    over FpiUsbTransfer, matcher in a GTask), suspend that keeps the
    running action and parks the capture at a transfer boundary, and USB
    autosuspend left at libfprint's default.
  * The no-autosuspend udev rule is gone, so debian/rules no longer
    installs it and the postinst no longer reloads udev for it. The
    driver was validated with power/control=auto.
  * The default matcher is the clean-room one. Templates do not survive
    the upgrade; users must re-enrol.

 -- PHILIPPDEV5396 <philipp2861@gmail.com>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:00:00 +0200

libfprint-egis0576 (1:1.94.100-99egis0.4.5) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Install the two integration files v0.4.5 added, so the Debian package
    ships the whole suspend/resume fix and not just half of it. Before
    this, only the systemd-sleep hook (which writes a claim marker to
    /run before fprintd stops) was installed, with nothing that reads the
    marker back, so the "Device was already claimed" race v0.4.5 fixed
    stayed open for Debian users. Mirrors packaging/fedora/libfprint.spec
    lines 96-103: the gate to /usr/libexec/egis0576-fp-wait (mode 0755)
    and the fprintd.service.d drop-in that runs it, to
    /usr/lib/systemd/system/fprintd.service.d/10-egis0576-resume-wait.conf
    (mode 0644).
  * Revision now carries the egis0576 driver tag (99egis0.4.5, was a
    hardcoded 99egis1), and .github/workflows/release.yml's verify-tag job
    now checks it the same way it already checks packaging/fedora's
    %global egis_tag and packaging/aur's #tag=. Before this change nothing
    verified the changelog revision at all, so every future tag would have
    kept shipping .debs stamped 99egis1: apt saw the same version on every
    release and treated `apt install ./*.deb` as a no-op reinstall,
    silently keeping users on whichever driver build they first installed.
      dpkg --compare-versions '1:1.94.100-99egis0.4.5' gt '1:1.94.9-1'   -> true
      dpkg --compare-versions '1:1.94.100-99egis0.4.5' gt '1:1.94.10-1'  -> true
      dpkg --compare-versions '1:1.94.100-99egis0.4.5' gt '1:1.94.100-1' -> true
      dpkg --compare-versions '...-99egis0.4.6' gt '...-99egis0.4.5'    -> true
    The epoch and the leading 99 still do the same job documented below:
    the epoch outranks Debian's own package, and 99 outranks any plausible
    normal Debian revision at the same epoch and upstream Version.
  * Maintainer address set to a working project mailbox. The previous value
    used the RFC 2606 reserved .invalid TLD, which never resolves. This
    field is carried by every .deb and .changes file, apt show displays it,
    and Launchpad mails PPA build failures to it, so it has to reach
    somebody. Upstream authorship stays credited in debian/copyright, which
    is the normal Debian split between packaging and upstream.
  * Rebuild of upstream libfprint v1.94.100 with the out-of-tree egis0576
    (EgisTec EH576, USB 1c7a:0576) driver compiled in. Mirrors
    packaging/fedora/libfprint.spec: same upstream version, same meson
    integration patch, same suspend/resume sleep hook and udev rule.
  * Epoch "1:" added. Debian trixie's stock libfprint-2-2 is 1:1.94.9-1 and
    sid/forky's is 1:1.94.10-1. An epoch always outranks the upstream
    version and the revision below it, so a build with no epoch sorts
    below both of those even though its own upstream version (1.94.100) is
    higher. Carrying the same epoch "1:" fixes that. No Provides, Conflicts
    or Replaces is needed as a result, and no install-time flag is needed
    either.

 -- Irving Popovetsky <libfprint-egis0576@popovetsky.com>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 09:00:00 +0000
